       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 30 mg is three times the usual maximum for amlodipine, which is 10 mg once daily. Doses above 10 mg aren't recommended. They raise the risk of significant hypotension, reflex tachycardia, peripheral edema, and, in overdose, prolonged vasodilatory shock. Your patient's normal kidney and liver function and lack of allergies don't change that ceiling.\n\nDid you mean one of these?\n- **10 mg PO once daily**, the maximum standard dose.\n- **5 mg PO once daily**, the usual starting dose, or 2.5 mg if you want a more cautious start.\n- **A different drug or combination** if you need more blood pressure control than 10 mg gives. For example, you could add an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide-like diuretic.\n\nIf 30 mg is intentional, tell me the clinical rationale and I'll enter it with a note documenting that you confirmed the dose. Pharmacy will probably flag it either way."
      }
